00:01:16,816 --> 00:01:19,397
input here. Still, this is no server. We're

31
00:01:19,397 --> 00:01:21,561
not writing any code that would run on a

32
00:01:21,561 --> 00:01:24,190
server and serve a website, but it is an

33
00:01:24,190 --> 00:01:26,787
important building block of Deno and how

34
00:01:26,835 --> 00:01:27,636
Deno works.

35
00:01:28,518 --> 00:01:31,372
Now to collect user input we have something

36
00:01:31,468 --> 00:01:33,458
on that global demo object which is called

37
00:01:33,554 --> 00:01:34,324
stdin.

38
00:01:35,014 --> 00:01:37,308
That stands for standard input in the end,

39
00:01:37,404 --> 00:01:39,442
and it gives us access to this standard

40
00:01:39,506 --> 00:01:42,474
input. And this terminal where we run our

41
00:01:42,474 --> 00:01:44,848
script is the standard input.

42
00:01:45,762 --> 00:01:48,089
Now here we have a read method which we can

43
00:01:48,089 --> 00:01:51,024
call, and this will basically read the user

44
00:01:51,056 --> 00:01:52,741
input and store it somewhere.

45
00:01:53,784 --> 00:01:56,399
Now that somewhere where it should be stored

46
00:01:56,976 --> 00:01:59,208
is something we have to pass to read. It

47
00:01:59,304 --> 00:02:02,097
wants a Uint8Array as an input.

48
00:02:02,916 --> 00:02:06,127
Uint8Array is this strange data structure

49
00:02:06,303 --> 00:02:08,310
which is already built into JavaScript,

50
00:02:08,631 --> 00:02:10,558
which in the end is an array that stores raw

51
00:02:10,879 --> 00:02:13,110
bytes. And we can simply create such an

52
00:02:13,110 --> 00:02:15,454
array here, maybe name it buffer, because

53
00:02:15,454 --> 00:02:17,926
we'll buffer— we'll collect all the user

54
00:02:17,942 --> 00:02:20,671
input before we then use it in one big

55
00:02:20,671 --> 00:02:22,903
chunk, so to say. We can create it by

56
00:02:22,903 --> 00:02:23,433
calling new

57
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:27,189
uint8 array that's built into Deno and

58
00:02:27,205 --> 00:02:29,067
available like this. It's actually built

59
00:02:29,067 --> 00:02:31,333
into JavaScript even. So it's available like

60
00:02:31,333 --> 00:02:31,609
this,

61
00:02:32,531 --> 00:02:33,987
and we can pass this buffer

62
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:37,323
to read. However, on this buffer, I'll set

63
00:02:37,372 --> 00:02:39,660
the length of the buffer, so basically how

64
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:41,433
much space for how many bytes I want to

65
00:02:41,481 --> 00:02:43,447
reserve. And I'll use 1024,

66
00:02:45,887 --> 00:02:47,802
which should allow for quite a long input

67
00:02:47,899 --> 00:02:48,172
message.

68
00:02:49,524 --> 00:02:51,745
So now it's this buffer which we pass to

69
00:02:52,164 --> 00:02:52,389
read.

70
00:02:53,647 --> 00:02:56,324
And the user input will be stored as bytes

71
00:02:56,693 --> 00:02:57,270
in that buffer.

72
00:02:58,136 --> 00:03:00,172
At the moment, if we would run this script,

73
00:03:00,316 --> 00:03:02,368
we would not be able to do anything with

74
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:03,185
that input though.

75
00:03:03,826 --> 00:03:06,279
As you can imagine, waiting for user input

76
00:03:06,391 --> 00:03:09,453
is an asynchronous task. We as a developer

77
00:03:09,453 --> 00:03:11,216
don't know in advance how long that will

78
00:03:11,216 --> 00:03:11,617
take.

79
00:03:12,338 --> 00:03:14,663
In the very next line after this read

80
00:03:14,663 --> 00:03:17,276
method, we'll not have the result yet

81
00:03:17,628 --> 00:03:19,520
because we have to wait until the input is

82
00:03:19,552 --> 00:03:21,796
finished. And as you know, JavaScript

83
00:03:21,796 --> 00:03:23,848
doesn't wait for this to complete. It moves

84
00:03:23,944 --> 00:03:25,196
on to the next line right away

85
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:28,360
and will not have that result in that next

86
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:28,585
line.

87
00:03:29,404 --> 00:03:32,022
But that's no problem because read returns a

88
00:03:32,134 --> 00:03:32,665
promise.

89
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:36,600
And a promise simply allows us to add this

90
00:03:37,130 --> 00:03:39,989
then method. And in the function we pass to

91
00:03:40,021 --> 00:03:40,213
then,

92
00:03:40,743 --> 00:03:42,558
there we'll have the user input.

93
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:43,699
However,

94
00:03:44,502 --> 00:03:46,831
we don't need to use then, of course. We can

95
00:03:46,847 --> 00:03:49,160
use async/await as you learned in the

96
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:52,465
JavaScript refresher. And in Deno, we can

97
00:03:52,529 --> 00:03:54,148
even use just await.

98
00:03:54,933 --> 00:03:58,075
Deno supports top-level await,

99
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:00,688
which means we don't have to wrap this in

100
00:04:00,736 --> 00:04:03,653
some async function first. We can just add

101
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:04,920
await in front of this.

102
00:04:05,449 --> 00:04:06,827
This is supported by Deno,

103
00:04:07,356 --> 00:04:11,155
though to be transparent, from version 14.3

104
00:04:11,284 --> 00:04:14,522
on, it's also supported in Node.js. In older

105
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:17,359
versions of Node.js, this was not supported

106
00:04:17,391 --> 00:04:19,573
though. So there you always needed that

107
00:04:19,621 --> 00:04:23,168
async function. In Node 13.3 and higher, it

108
00:04:23,232 --> 00:04:25,543
is supported, and in Deno it's supported as

109
00:04:25,655 --> 00:04:25,768
well.

110
00:04:27,116 --> 00:04:30,438
So now basically the next line will only run

111
00:04:30,614 --> 00:04:32,941
after this line completed, because behind

112
00:04:32,941 --> 00:04:35,653
the scenes, as you know and as you learned

113
00:04:35,750 --> 00:04:38,462
in the refresher module, behind the scenes

114
00:04:38,799 --> 00:04:41,864
when you use await, this is transformed to a

115
00:04:41,864 --> 00:04:44,560
promise, and any code you add after await is

116
00:04:44,592 --> 00:04:46,642
simply added to the function that you would

117
00:04:46,755 --> 00:04:48,453
otherwise pass to the then method.

118
00:04:49,494 --> 00:04:50,488
So here we can then

119
00:04:51,144 --> 00:04:53,243
output the user input and for that we can

120
00:04:53,243 --> 00:04:54,365
create a decoder.

121
00:04:55,230 --> 00:04:58,130
Previously in our first example, we created

122
00:04:58,194 --> 00:05:01,831
an encoder to basically convert a string to

123
00:05:01,831 --> 00:05:05,067
such a Uint8Array. So to an array full of

124
00:05:05,099 --> 00:05:06,573
bytes, that's what we did before.

125
00:05:07,486 --> 00:05:10,514
Now we have the opposite use case. Now we're

126
00:05:10,530 --> 00:05:12,533
getting an array full of bytes and we wanna

127
00:05:12,533 --> 00:05:14,359
convert it to a string which we can output.

128
00:05:15,113 --> 00:05:17,328
And that's where the decoder helps us. We

129
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:20,313
can create a new TextDecoder that's also

130
00:05:20,313 --> 00:05:23,041
available just like this in Deno. We don't

131
00:05:23,041 --> 00:05:25,417
need to import anything to use this API

132
00:05:25,465 --> 00:05:25,689
here.

133
00:05:26,508 --> 00:05:28,562
And we can then get our input by calling

134
00:05:28,739 --> 00:05:31,595
decoder.decode and passing in

135
00:05:32,510 --> 00:05:35,142
our buffer. Keep in mind that the buffer is

136
00:05:35,495 --> 00:05:36,907
where the input will be saved to.

137
00:05:37,918 --> 00:05:40,647
So as a final step, let's then console.log

138
00:05:41,112 --> 00:05:41,929
the input here

139
00:05:42,795 --> 00:05:44,174
to see what the user entered.

140
00:05:44,879 --> 00:05:47,636
And with that, I'm going to deno run my

141
00:05:47,668 --> 00:05:50,506
deno_input.ts file and see what that gives

142
00:05:50,554 --> 00:05:50,634
us.

143
00:05:52,093 --> 00:05:54,721
And as you see, it compiles and it does not

144
00:05:54,721 --> 00:05:56,934
finish. It's not going back to the initial

145
00:05:57,527 --> 00:06:00,108
terminal which had my computer name at the

